<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' }, { label: 'Identity & OAuth' }]}>
  <h1>Identity &amp; OAuth</h1>

  <p>
    OpenFlows acts on GitHub through a <strong>real identity</strong>, not a shared token. When the team
    pushes a branch, opens a pull request, or comments on an issue, it does so as the tenant's linked
    GitHub identity, resolved through a <strong>scoped OAuth link</strong>. This page explains that
    model in user-facing terms.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="No raw tokens in worker workspaces">
    This is the single most important fact about how OpenFlows authenticates: <strong>worker
    workspaces hold no raw repository credentials and no model keys.</strong> The working spaces where
    agents do their jobs are thin and disposable - they contain none of the secrets that would let an
    attacker - or a misbehaving agent - act on your behalf.
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="1" id="identity-model">The identity model</StepHeading>
  <p>
    OpenFlows never relies on a shared token sitting inside a sandbox. Instead, identity works in two
    layers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A scoped GitHub OAuth link per tenant.</strong> For each tenant, you link a real GitHub
      identity through OAuth in the Coder dashboard (or the OpenFlows control panel). This grants the
      team the scoped ability to read the repository, push branches, open pull requests, and comment on
      issues - attributable to that tenant's identity, not to a shared, mysterious account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A real, attributable identity for every action.</strong> Every action an agent takes
      inherits a real identity through Coder's login and that scoped OAuth link. There are no shared,
      anonymous accounts. When something is merged or commented on, it is attributable and auditable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <StepHeading step="2" id="where-credentials-live">Where credentials live</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Credentials are deliberately concentrated, not spread around:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The control plane</strong> is where model access and the coordination happen. Every AI
      call is routed centrally through the model gateway, so worker workspaces never hold a model key.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The orchestrator workspace</strong> is the one long-lived, trusted component that
      legitimately holds the credentials the workers never see. It runs the coordination loop and is
      the auditable place where trust is concentrated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Worker workspaces</strong> hold none of this. They have no raw repository credentials, no
      model keys, and no secrets - only the repository checkout, the coordination surface, and their
      role's skills.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="tip" title="Why this keeps things safe">
    Remove the secrets from the sandbox and an entire class of "stolen key" attacks disappears. Trust
    is concentrated in a single auditable place, and everything else is disposable and keyless. See the
    <a href="/docs/concepts/security-model">Security Model</a> page for the full picture.
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="3" id="how-it-is-set-up">How it is set up</StepHeading>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Add a tenant</strong> for your repository (see <a href="/docs/getting-started/first-issue">First Issue Walkthrough</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Complete GitHub OAuth</strong> for that tenant - link the real identity OpenFlows will act as.</li>
    <li>That scope follows the team from then on. No per-ticket token management is needed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If a deeper topic (such as token-level configuration or troubleshooting) is not covered here, that
    is because the current documentation keeps the authentication model intentionally simple: a scoped
    OAuth link, real identity, and no raw tokens in workspaces.
  </p>

